<header><div><h1>Indian Tribal Women Writers Archive</h1><p>Digital cultural directory · source-checked research edition</p></div><span class="tag">Source-led edition · November 2026</span></header>
<section class="hero"><span class="tag">Preserving Indigenous voices &amp; literary histories</span><h2>Tribal and Adivasi Women Writers of India</h2><p>A source-linked directory of 33 women writers, poets, storytellers and literary contributors from different Indigenous communities and regions of India. Entries are limited to claims supported by the cited research or institutional records.</p><div class="stats"><span><strong id="count">33</strong><br>verified profiles</span><span><strong>Source-linked</strong><br>biographical claims</span><span><strong>0</strong><br>unattributed quotations</span></div></section>
<main class="wrap">
<div class="notice"><strong>Editorial correction notice</strong><br>This edition contains 33 profiles drawn from an academic Adivasi-writers project, an IWGIA publication, Sahitya Akademi’s All India Tribal Women Writers’ Meet report, and an academic study of Bodo women’s writing. Entries supported only by group lists are clearly described as such; a group-list citation confirms inclusion as a writer but does not establish a complete biography, book list or place of birth. Unverified quotations, book titles and publishers are not included.</div>
<div class="toolbar"><input id="search" type="search" placeholder="Search by writer, language, community, state or role…" aria-label="Search writers"><select id="language" aria-label="Filter by language"><option value="">All language descriptions</option></select><select id="state" aria-label="Filter by state"><option value="">All state descriptions</option></select></div>
<div class="meta"><span id="result-count">Showing 33 profiles</span><span class="small">Each profile links to the source that supports the listed information.</span></div>
<section id="directory" class="grid" aria-live="polite"></section>
<div class="method"><h2 class="section-title" style="margin-top:0">Research and verification policy</h2><ul><li><b>Evidence-led sample:</b> this edition contains 33 distinct writer profiles, each with at least one traceable source link.</li><li><b>Source transparency:</b> each entry links to the source that identifies the writer. A source link is provided on every profile. Where a source only supports a short description, the profile remains brief and does not add unsupported biography details.</li><li><b>No assumed bibliography:</b> titles, dates and publishers are included only when the cited source supports them. This edition does not invent book lists or quotations.</li><li><b>State and language caution:</b> profiles without a source-supported state or regional association have been removed from this edition.</li><li><b>Terminology:</b> “tribal” and “Adivasi” follow the language used by the cited source. Community labels are reproduced cautiously and may vary across sources.</li><li><b>Not exhaustive:</b> the directory is a research sample, not a complete census of Indigenous women’s writing in India. More profiles can be added when individual-level evidence is available.</li></ul><p class="small">The 33 entries are distinct names, not 60 books or quotations. For group-roster entries, the linked source confirms the person’s inclusion as a literary contributor but may not provide a full biography.</p></div>
<h2 class="section-title">Core references</h2><ol class="source-list"><li><a href="https://scalar.lehigh.edu/adivasi-writers-indias-indigenous-literary-tradition/list-of-adivasi-writers.75" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lehigh University — Adivasi Writers: an inclusive list of writers</a> (writer profiles and literary descriptions).</li><li><a href="https://iwgia.org/en/documents-and-publications/documents/publications-pdfs/english-publications/128-book-indigenous-heroines-final-draft-dec-2015/file.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IWGIA — A Saga of Tribal Women of India</a> (2015; identifies tribal women authors, critics and poets across communities).</li><li><a href="https://sahitya-akademi.gov.in/aboutus/pdf/AR-2018-19.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sahitya Akademi — Annual Report 2018–2019</a> (official record of the All India Tribal Women Writers’ Meet and participants).</li><li><a href="https://www.sahitya-akademi.gov.in/aboutus/pdf/AR-2019-20.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sahitya Akademi — Annual Report 2019–2020</a> (official listing of noted Santali and Bodo women writers).</li><li><a href="https://www.ijnrd.org/papers/IJNRD2408170.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Women Writers in Bodo Literature and Their Philosophy</a> (academic article naming women short-story writers, prose writers and critics).</li><li><a href="https://hydlitfest.org/speaker/vasamalli/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hyderabad Literary Festival — Vasamalli Kurtaz</a> (Toda community and literary work).</li><li><a href="https://www.sahapedia.org/gondi-language-and-literature-conversation-ushakiran-atram" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sahapedia — Ushakiran Atram</a> (Gondi poetry and literary work).</li><li><a href="https://indianwritinginenglish.uohyd.ac.in/mamang_dai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">University of Hyderabad — Mamang Dai</a> (Arunachal Pradesh writer profile).</li></ol>
